    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Glass and purple dye were Sidon's two pivotal industries; a considerable mound of broken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrian-purple">Murex</a> trunculus shells near the southern harbour shows the scale of the dye works.</li><li>Sidon's navy fought with the Persian fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-salamis">Battle of Salamis</a> in 480 BCE, and from the mid-5th century BCE warships were a prominent feature of the city's coins.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> rewarded Sidon for supporting his conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with the territories of Dor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Joppa</a> and the Plain of Sharon.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The oldest testimony to words of the Phoenician language of Sidon is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-deuteronomy">Book of Deuteronomy</a>, which records that the Sidonians called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hermon">Mount Hermon</a> 'Sirion'.</p></aside>
    </section>

