nchor="middle">5</text><title>5th century BCE: Eshmunazar II reigns as 'king of the Sidonians'; his sarcophagus, found in 1855, names Eshmun and Baal Sidon as the chief gods of the city.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="728.3" x2="728.3" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="728.3" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="728.3" y="164" text-anchor="middle">6</text><title>351 BCE: Artaxerxes III invades Phoenicia at the end of the Persian era.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="739.4" x2="739.4" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="739.4" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="739.4" y="164" text-anchor="middle">7</text><title>333 BCE: Alexander the Great occupies Sidon, beginning its Hellenistic period.</title></g><text class="rf-axis" x="20" y="200">1500 BCE</text><text class="rf-axis" x="740" y="200" text-anchor="end">332 BCE</text></svg><figcaption>Relative power on the Institute's 0–10 scale (each profile scaled to its own peak). Numbers mark the key events below.</figcaption></figure><ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1350 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Sidon, part of the Egyptian Empire, is ruled by Zimredda during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> period, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s hold on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> weakens and the coastal cities fight for dominance.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">7th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshmun">Eshmun</a>, to the Phoenician god of healing, is built north of the city near the Awali River.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">6th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>'s subjection of the city to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a>, Sidon passes to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">480 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Sidon's navy plays a significant role at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-salamis">Battle of Salamis</a>, aligned with the Persian fleet against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">5th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshmunazar-ii">Eshmunazar II</a> reigns as 'king of the Sidonians'; his sarcophagus, found in 1855, names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshmun">Eshmun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> Sidon as the chief gods of the city.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">351 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii">Artaxerxes III</a> invades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> at the end of the Persian era.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">333 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> occupies Sidon, beginning its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>.</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amarna-letters.jpg" alt="Amarna letters" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Five_Amarna_letters_on_display_at_the_British_Museum,_LondonA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sidon sits on a mainland promontory of the Lebanese coast about 40 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> to the south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> to the north, with two harbours. Its Phoenician name, Sidun, probably meant 'fishery' or 'fishing town', Hellenised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as Sidon. People lived here from the deepest prehistory: the Sidon II site holds Acheulean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> and Sidon III a Heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> assemblage from just before pottery. The tell of the ancient city, Sidon IV, lies beneath the ruined Castle of St Louis, with an unbroken sequence from Early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> I to III on bedrock from about 3200 BCE and a modest third-millennium settlement of houses and tombs.</p><p>In the late Middle Bronze IIA the port, Sidon Level 4, traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> of the late 12th and early 13th dynasties, and excavations at Tell el-Burak, 9 km to the south, have shown Sidon to have been the great power of the coast in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a>, a 'Kingdom of Sidon' controlling el-Burak and much surrounding territory. The city first appears in historical texts in the middle of the second millennium BCE. By about 1350 BCE it belonged to the Egyptian Empire and was ruled by Zimredda of Sidon, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> record the rivalry of the coastal cities, Abimilku of Tyre to the south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rib-hadda">Rib-Hadda</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> to the north, as Egypt's grip loosened and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> became overlords in the north. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a> Sidon is the first-born son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tyrian-purple"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tyrian-purple.jpg" alt="Tyrian purple" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tyrian-purple">Tyrian purple</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Purple_Purpur_(retouched).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photograph: U.Name.Me; derivative work by TeKaBe, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sidon was one of the most important Phoenician cities and may have been the oldest; from it and the other ports a great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> commercial empire was founded, and tradition held that a colonising party from Sidon went out to found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, which grew into a rival, each claiming to be the metropolis, the mother city, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> praised the skill of its craftsmen in glass and purple dye and the embroidery of its women. Two industries carried the city: glass was made on an extensive scale, and purple dye nearly matched it in importance. The dye came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrian-purple">Murex</a> trunculus shells, broken open to extract a pigment so rare that it became synonymous with royalty, and a considerable mound of the discarded shells was found near the southern harbour.</p><p>The city's maritime strength showed in its coinage, on which warships became a prominent feature from the mid-5th century BCE. The Phoenician inscription on the sarcophagus of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshmunazar-ii">Eshmunazar II</a>, discovered in 1855, calls him 'king of the Sidonians' and says his mother was a priestess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Ashtart</a>, 'the goddess of the Sidonians', naming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshmun">Eshmun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> Sidon, 'Lord of Sidon', as the chief gods and giving Ashtart the title Ashtart-Shem-Baal, 'Ashtart the name of the Lord', a title also found in an Ugaritic text. The Temple of Eshmun, to the god of healing, was built in the 7th century BCE north of the city near the Awali River.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii.jpg" alt="Nebuchadnezzar II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nebuchadnezzar_II_crop.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sidon's power lay on the water, and its history is one of conquerors. Throughout antiquity it fell in turn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>; its position on a promontory with two harbours did not save it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>, who made it part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a>, nor from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, which took the Phoenician cities in the 6th century BCE. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> age, around 1350 BCE, the Lebanese coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> fought for dominance as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> declined: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkar">Akkar</a> Plain rebelled with Hittite support to become the kingdom of Amurru, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni Empire</a> that had dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> fell apart under the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i">Suppiluliuma I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatti">Hatti</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a> and his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horemheb">Horemheb</a> scrambled to keep Egypt's hold on the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>.</p><p>The Sidonian fleet was the city's chief contribution to its overlords' wars. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-salamis">Battle of Salamis</a> in 480 BCE its navy played a significant role on the Persian side against the Greeks, and from the middle of the 5th century BCE warships were stamped on its coins. Such service had its reward: for supporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a>'s conquest of Egypt the Persian king granted Sidon Dor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Joppa</a> and the Plain of Sharon. At the end of the Persian era, in 351 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii">Artaxerxes III</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>, and in 333 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> occupied the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/eshmunazar-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eshmunazar-ii.jpg" alt="Eshmunazar II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/eshmunazar-ii">Eshmunazar II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eshmunazar_II_sarcophagus_(31289612892).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fred Romero from Paris, France, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sidon was a kingdom under its own kings for most of its ancient history, whatever empire stood over it. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> period its ruler Zimredda answered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>; under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> a Sidonian king still reigned, and the sarcophagus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshmunazar-ii">Eshmunazar II</a>, probably of the 5th century BCE, shows kingship and the priesthood of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Ashtart</a> joined in one family. Persian influence was profound and can be seen in the changed architectural style of the city; under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> Sidon eclipsed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> to become the paramount city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> enlarged its territory with Dor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Joppa</a> and the Plain of Sharon. Under Alexander's successors it enjoyed relative autonomy.</p><p>The Hellenistic necropolis has yielded the Alexander Sarcophagus, likely the tomb of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdalonymus">Abdalonymus</a> rather than of Alexander, the Lycian tomb and the Sarcophagus of the Crying Women, all now in the Archaeological Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. Under Roman domination the city kept minting its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins; Herod, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>, built it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, and in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elagabalus">Elagabalus</a> it became a Roman colony, renamed Colonia Aurelia Pia Sidon in honour of its imperial sponsor. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, which calls Sidon the mother city of Tyre and says the Sidonians long oppressed Israel, records <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s marriage alliance with the Sidonians and names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezebel">Jezebel</a> a daughter of King Ithobaal I of Sidon. In Byzantine times, after the earthquake of 551 CE, the law school of Berytus took refuge in the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/astarte"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/astarte.jpg" alt="Astarte" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Astarte_-_7th_cent_AD_-_Museo_Arqueol%C3%B3gico_de_Sevilla-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ismoon 21:17, 4 January 2022 (UTC), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From Sidon and its sister ports the Phoenician commercial world spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and Sidon's own claim to be the metropolis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> rested on the tradition that its colonists founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>. Its glass and its purple, the colour of royalty, made its name a byword in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, and its gods travelled: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Ashtart</a>'s title at Sidon matches one found at Ugarit. The Bible remembers the city as the first home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, 'great' from its commercial relations, famous for its manufactures and arts, and from the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> waning as Tyre, its 'virgin daughter', rose; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a> sojourned there, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> visited the coasts of Tyre and Sidon, and Paul's ship put in at Sidon on the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><p>Sidon's fleet weighed in the wars of empires at Salamis, and its favour with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> shows how the Persian kings used the Phoenician cities. In the Hellenistic age it gave the Greek world <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> of Sidon, the poet, Zeno of Sidon, the Epicurean philosopher, Dorotheus of Sidon, the astrologer, and Boethus of Sidon, the Peripatetic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-renan">Ernest Renan</a>'s Mission de Phénicie of 1860 and the Ottoman excavations from 1887 made its necropoleis central to the recovery of Phoenician antiquity, and the royal sarcophagi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and the finds in the National Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> remain its best-known legacy. In 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> gave the archaeological sites of Sidon enhanced protection.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="Alexander the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Mosaic_detail_of_Alexander_the_Great_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The independent Phoenician city ended with the Persian Empire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxerxes-iii">Artaxerxes III</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> in 351 BCE, and in 333 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> occupied Sidon, opening its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diadochi">Diadochi</a> the city kept a relative autonomy, with kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdalonymus">Abdalonymus</a>, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> it prospered, embellished by Herod with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, sheltering enough <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in 66 CE that local pagans hesitated to attack them, and raised to colonial rank under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elagabalus">Elagabalus</a>. The earthquake of 551 CE destroyed most of the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenice">Phoenice</a>, and in 636 the Muslim conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> took the city.</p><p>Sidon's later centuries were a cycle of capture and ruin. On 4 December 1110 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin I of Jerusalem</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigurd-the-crusader">Sigurd I of Norway</a> took it after a siege and made it the centre of the Lordship of Sidon; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> captured it in 1187, German crusaders restored it in 1197, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> destroyed it in 1249 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a> in 1260. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> from the early 16th century it became capital of the Sidon Eyalet and regained much of its commerce, the Hammud family monopolising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> in the 18th century; Charles Napier's squadron bombarded it on 26 September 1840. Today Sidon, or Saida, is the third-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and capital of the South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>, with some 80,000 people in the city and more than a quarter of a million in its metropolitan area; the ancient basin is a fishing port, and the tell of Phoenician Sidon lies under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> castle.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Sidon, a Phoenician port with two harbours, grew from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a> kingdom trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> into one of the great cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>, famed for glass and purple dye; its fleet served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> at Salamis in 480 BCE, and it became the paramount Phoenician city before Alexander took it in 333 BCE.</em></p>

